      "history_summaries": [
        "Round 1/1: Fix DUT bug — changed `bus_gnt` from `RegInit(0.U(2.W))` to `Wire(UInt(2.W))` (line 199 in twoqLTLM1.scala). Root cause: the registered `bus_gnt` created a one-cycle overhang where the grant signal persisted for one extra cycle after the winning queue deasserted its request. This caused all five failing assertions (`buechi_progress_on_match`, `gnt0_only_when_q0_req`, `gnt1_only_when_q1_req`, `select_high_grants_q1`, `select_low_grants_q0`). With a combinatorial Wire, the arbiter decision is immediate: bus_gnt reflects the current arbitration conditions, eliminating the overhang. Assertion labels preserved unchanged. No homologous assertions needed (error_type=dut_bug).",
        "Round 2/2: Fix DUT bug in sampleq class — removed unconditional `storeaddrReg := readheadReg` on line 63 (previously line 63, now removed) from the `when(io.bus_gnt)` block. Root cause: on every bus grant cycle, `storeaddrReg` was unconditionally overwritten with the current `readheadReg` value, destroying the stored match address that the Buechi automaton relies on to detect that `storeaddr != readhead` (i.e., progress past a match). Without the unconditional line, `storeaddrReg` retains its old value when no new match is found, so when the readhead has advanced past the matched entry, `storeaddr != readhead` stays true and the Buechi in s_n1 correctly transitions to s_n3 (progress) instead of s_Trap. The conditional `storeaddrReg := readheadReg` inside the for-loop's `when` block on each match is preserved, so storeaddr is correctly updated on new matches. Target assertion `buechi_progress_on_match` label preserved unchanged. No homologous assertions needed (error_type=dut_bug — only this one unconditional assignment in sampleq).",
        "Round 3/3: Fix DUT bug — changed `writehead < writetail` to `writehead <= writetail` on line 66 of twoqLTLM1.scala (inside sampleq class, `writeEntryValid` computation in the `when(io.bus_gnt)` for-loop). Root cause: when `writehead == writetail` (write FIFO empty), the condition `writehead < writetail` is false, causing the Mux to take the wrapped-case branch `(i.U >= writehead) || (i.U < writetail)` which evaluates to `true` for `i == writehead` (e.g., `i=0, writehead=0, writetail=0` → `true`). This falsely marks stale write FIFO entries as valid, triggering a false match between the read head entry and an unwritten write FIFO slot (`readfifo(0)=00 === writefifo(0)=00`). The false match sets `matchReg=true` and `storeaddrReg=readheadReg`, and when readheadReg subsequently increments past the matched entry, `storeaddr != readhead` causes the Buechi automaton to take the Trap path instead of progressing to s_n3. Fix: `writehead <= writetail` ensures the empty-FIFO case (equal pointers) takes the first branch `(i >= writehead) && (i < writetail)` which correctly evaluates to `false` for all `i` since `i < writetail = writehead` is never true. This is a DUT causality fix addressing the causal path: the false `writeEntryValid` is what triggers the spurious match that ultimately causes the Buechi trap and assertion failure. Assertion label `buechi_progress_on_match` preserved unchanged. No homologous assertions needed (error_type=dut_bug — only this one `writeEntryValid` computation in sampleq)."
      ],
